WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
In the mutagens video, you saw these numbers:

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.440
a hundred and forty two yeast colonies with

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:08.920
no UV, and just five after two minutes.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.600
And you saw that fewer colonies doesn't prove

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:12.880
more mutants.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:14.240
But why not?

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:17.040
If some cells mutated and survived, where would

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:18.000
they be?

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:20.960
They'd be hiding inside the colonies that grew.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:23.680
So what can colony counts really tell you?

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:26.980
First, what's a colony?

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.540
Spread about a hundred and fifty yeast cells

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.180
thinly on agar, and each cell that can

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:34.740
still divide makes a pile of millions of

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:36.580
copies in two days.

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:38.980
That pile is a colony, big enough to

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:39.940
see.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:42.340
Each colony grew from a surviving cell or

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:44.900
small clump that kept dividing.

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:47.300
A cell that can't divide leaves nothing to

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:47.700
see.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:52.340
Now add UV, which joins neighbouring thymines, as

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:52.900
you saw.

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:55.780
Each cell then has one of four fates.

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:58.180
No damage: it grows a colony.

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:00.900
Damaged, but repaired: a colony.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:04.900
Too much damage: copying stalls at many dimers,

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:07.060
so the cell can't divide, and there's no

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:07.620
colony.

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:11.140
Or a changed base gets copied, the cell

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:13.300
lives, and it grows a colony that looks

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:14.500
just like the others.

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:17.780
So the count measures survival, and any mutants

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:19.300
are counted as survivors.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:23.460
In this practical, a teacher collects the data,

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:24.660
not students.

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:27.220
The lamp gives out UVC, and it can

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:29.620
burn skin and injure eyes after a short

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:30.820
exposure.

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:33.460
So the plates go into an interlocked cabinet,

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:35.620
which switches the lamp off whenever the door

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:36.740
opens.

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:39.300
Your job in this practical is the analysis.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:45.140
The independent variable is exposure time: zero, fifteen,

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:48.300
thirty, sixty and a hundred and twenty seconds.

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:51.060
The dependent variable is the number of colonies

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:53.780
after forty eight hours at thirty degrees.

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:56.500
Everything else stays the same: the same yeast

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:59.860
suspension, the same hundred microlitres on each plate,

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:02.420
the same lamp at the same twenty centimetres,

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:03.860
and the same incubation.

00:02:05.067 --> 00:02:07.466
The zero second plates are the control.

00:02:07.707 --> 00:02:09.626
They go into the cabinet too, with their

00:02:09.667 --> 00:02:11.706
lids off for the same time, but the

00:02:11.787 --> 00:02:12.826
lamp stays off.

00:02:13.467 --> 00:02:16.026
Then the only difference between a control plate

00:02:16.107 --> 00:02:18.346
and an exposed plate is the UV.

00:02:18.987 --> 00:02:21.306
Leave the controls on the bench, and handling

00:02:21.387 --> 00:02:22.826
could explain the difference.

00:02:24.034 --> 00:02:26.673
Each time has three plates, made the same

00:02:26.754 --> 00:02:27.073
way.

00:02:27.634 --> 00:02:29.873
At zero seconds, they grew a hundred and

00:02:29.914 --> 00:02:32.353
thirty three, a hundred and forty four and

00:02:32.434 --> 00:02:35.233
a hundred and forty nine colonies, a mean

00:02:35.314 --> 00:02:37.313
of a hundred and forty two.

00:02:37.354 --> 00:02:40.513
They don't match exactly, because each hundred microlitres

00:02:40.554 --> 00:02:43.193
picks up a slightly different number of cells.

00:02:43.394 --> 00:02:44.993
The mean evens that out.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:48.920
To compare the times, turn each mean into

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:52.000
a relative survival: the mean divided by the

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:54.120
control mean, times a hundred.

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:57.080
At thirty seconds, sixty one over a hundred

00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:59.640
and forty two gives forty three point zero

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:00.760
percent.

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:03.320
After two minutes, it's just three point five

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:04.040
percent.

00:03:05.267 --> 00:03:07.986
Graph exposure time on the x axis and

00:03:08.067 --> 00:03:10.146
relative survival on the y.

00:03:10.467 --> 00:03:12.946
Plot each mean, and show every plate as

00:03:12.987 --> 00:03:15.346
a small point, so the spread is visible.

00:03:16.067 --> 00:03:19.106
Survival falls steeply at first, then levels off

00:03:19.187 --> 00:03:20.066
towards zero.

00:03:20.627 --> 00:03:23.266
So longer exposure to this lamp, at this

00:03:23.347 --> 00:03:25.826
distance, went with lower survival.

00:03:27.034 --> 00:03:29.073
So what do the data support?

00:03:29.354 --> 00:03:32.313
That UVC exposure reduced the survival of these

00:03:32.394 --> 00:03:34.633
yeast cells under these conditions.

00:03:34.954 --> 00:03:36.153
What don't they show?

00:03:36.554 --> 00:03:38.713
Whether any survivors mutated.

00:03:38.954 --> 00:03:41.193
The missing colonies tell you about cells that

00:03:41.274 --> 00:03:43.993
couldn't divide, and the mutants, if there are

00:03:44.074 --> 00:03:46.313
any, are among the cells that lived.

00:03:47.534 --> 00:03:50.253
To measure mutation, you'd test the survivors for

00:03:50.294 --> 00:03:52.173
a new trait that's inherited.

00:03:52.494 --> 00:03:55.533
Mutants are rare, so spread millions of exposed

00:03:55.614 --> 00:03:57.933
cells on a medium that normal yeast can't

00:03:58.054 --> 00:04:00.173
grow on, like the antibiotic dish in the

00:04:00.254 --> 00:04:01.213
first video.

00:04:01.534 --> 00:04:03.613
Count the colonies that grow as a fraction

00:04:03.654 --> 00:04:07.213
of survivors, and compare it with unexposed cells.

00:04:07.414 --> 00:04:09.773
Then grow those colonies again, to check the

00:04:09.894 --> 00:04:10.973
trait is passed on.

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:15.560
Validity: with one variable changed and a control

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:18.680
handled the same way, the counts validly compare

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:21.160
survival, but they aren't a valid measure of

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:21.880
mutation.

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:26.040
Reliability: the three plates at each time differ

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:28.840
only as much as chance predicts, like fifty

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:30.520
five to sixty seven at thirty

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:31.720
seconds.

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:34.560
Accuracy: count through the lid on a grid,

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:36.040
with a second counter.

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:39.080
And time alone isn't dose: to know the

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:41.880
dose, measure the lamp's output at the plate.

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:44.500
The risk assessment.

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:45.500
UVC:

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:48.940
only trained staff run the interlocked cabinet, and

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:50.940
the lamp is never on with the door

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:51.660
open.

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:54.700
The yeast is a risk group one organism,

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:57.740
unlikely to cause disease, but other microbes can

00:04:57.820 --> 00:04:59.340
grow on the plates too.

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:02.300
So grown plates stay sealed and are sterilised

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:03.820
before disposal.

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:06.460
The bench is wiped with seventy percent ethanol,

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:08.700
which is flammable, so keep it away from

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:09.420
flames.

00:05:10.634 --> 00:05:11.193
Your turn.

00:05:11.274 --> 00:05:14.473
In a repeat of this investigation, the zero

00:05:14.554 --> 00:05:17.273
second plates grew a hundred and forty five,

00:05:17.354 --> 00:05:19.433
a hundred and fifty one and a hundred

00:05:19.514 --> 00:05:21.033
and sixty colonies.

00:05:21.354 --> 00:05:23.913
The plates exposed for forty five seconds grew

00:05:24.074 --> 00:05:27.193
thirty six, forty one and forty six.

00:05:27.514 --> 00:05:30.913
Calculate the relative survival at forty five seconds.

00:05:31.194 --> 00:05:32.553
Pause here to answer.

00:05:42.967 --> 00:05:45.126
The control mean is four hundred and fifty

00:05:45.287 --> 00:05:48.486
six over three, a hundred and fifty two.

00:05:48.567 --> 00:05:50.566
The forty five second mean is a hundred

00:05:50.647 --> 00:05:53.766
and twenty three over three, forty one.

00:05:53.887 --> 00:05:56.086
Forty one over a hundred and fifty two,

00:05:56.207 --> 00:05:58.726
times a hundred, is twenty seven point zero

00:05:58.807 --> 00:05:59.846
percent.

00:05:59.887 --> 00:06:02.566
Seventy three percent is the trap: that's the

00:06:02.647 --> 00:06:04.566
share that didn't form colonies.

00:06:05.767 --> 00:06:06.406
One more.

00:06:06.967 --> 00:06:08.886
To have more colonies to count at a

00:06:08.927 --> 00:06:11.846
hundred and twenty seconds, a student spread those

00:06:11.927 --> 00:06:14.486
plates with a yeast suspension ten times as

00:06:14.567 --> 00:06:16.246
concentrated as all the others.

00:06:16.887 --> 00:06:18.806
The control mean was a hundred and forty

00:06:18.887 --> 00:06:21.286
two, and the hundred and twenty second mean

00:06:21.447 --> 00:06:22.406
was fifty two.

00:06:23.047 --> 00:06:25.686
She reported a relative survival of thirty six

00:06:25.847 --> 00:06:26.966
point six percent.

00:06:27.527 --> 00:06:28.806
Evaluate her result.

00:06:29.367 --> 00:06:30.646
Pause here to answer.

00:06:41.067 --> 00:06:43.706
As reported, her result isn't valid.

00:06:44.107 --> 00:06:46.266
The hundred and twenty second plates got ten

00:06:46.347 --> 00:06:49.226
times as many cells, so two things changed

00:06:49.307 --> 00:06:50.186
at once.

00:06:50.507 --> 00:06:53.226
Fifty two colonies there match about five point

00:06:53.307 --> 00:06:55.386
two on a standard plate, which is a

00:06:55.467 --> 00:06:58.346
survival of about three point seven percent, close

00:06:58.387 --> 00:06:59.306
to the main data.

00:06:59.867 --> 00:07:02.666
Her mistake was comparing plates given different numbers

00:07:02.747 --> 00:07:04.986
of cells without correcting for it.

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:10.040
So, to analyse a survival investigation: change one

00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:13.480
variable, handle the control the same way, use

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:17.400
repeats, turn means into relative survival, and graph

00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:18.360
every plate.

00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:22.360
Then claim only what you measured: colony counts

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:24.600
measure survival, not mutation.

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:28.040
Next: what one changed base can do to

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:29.000
a protein.

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:31.640
Here are the key notes.

00:07:31.880 --> 00:07:33.800
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:34.680
to copy them.
